<Doc>
    <Code language="ts" caption="ts" code={`import { testRows } from '@abide/harness/runner'

await testRows(
    [
        { section: 'app', root: 'src', runner: 'bun' },
        {
            section: 'browser',
            root: 'e2e',
            runner: 'playwright',
            projects: ['chromium'],
        },
    ],
    process.cwd(),
)`} />

    <Code language="sh" caption="terminal" code={`bun scripts/testRows.ts --list
bun scripts/testRows.ts --scope all --filter checkout`} />

    <Section title="A runner prints one row per line">
        <p><code>testRows</code> runs each test file in a process of its own and prints a
        <code>TestRow</code> as each one finishes: its counts, its time, and every
        <code>TestCase</code> with the failure its report gave. A file whose runner wrote no report
        is <code>broken</code>, which is a different thing from a red result, because a suite that
        hangs reports nothing at all.</p>

        <p><code>--list</code> prints the catalog as one JSON array of <code>RowItem</code>s.
        Anything else runs what <code>--scope</code> and <code>--filter</code> select —
        <code>all</code>, <code>section:&lt;name&gt;</code> or <code>item:&lt;id&gt;</code>, and a
        substring of the section and the name. <code>--gates</code> runs every gate's revert as
        well.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Your own runner, with the same command line">
        <p><code>rowsCommand</code> is that command line for any list: it takes the items and a
        function that runs the selected ones, and the function hands each row to
        <code>emit</code>. A bench runner prints a <code>BenchRow</code> per bench: the figure for
        your code, the hand-written one, the claim between them, the batch size and the floor.</p>

        <p><code>inScope</code> and <code>matchesFilter</code> are the selection, so a page showing
        the catalog filters it with the same function the runner selects with, and "run what I can
        see" runs exactly the rows on screen.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page reads the rows as they land">
        <p>The rows are a stream, so a page reads them one line at a time and patches each into
        what it already shows. A runner that dies part way leaves a last line that is a
        <code>RunnerFailure</code>, and <code>isRunnerFailure</code> tells it apart from a row.</p>

        <p>This site's Bench and Tests tabs are built this way, from these runners, over the
        repository the site is built from.</p>
    </Section>
</Doc>
